def solve(case):\\n    p = case['payload']\\n    sv = p['sentence_vectors']\\n    qv = p['query_vector']\\n    cw = p['context_weight']\\n    if sv:\\n        mean_vec = [sum(col)/len(sv) for col in zip(*sv)]\\n    else:\\n        mean_vec = [0.0]*len(qv) if qv else []\\n    blended = [(1-cw)*q + cw*m for q, m in zip(qv, mean_vec)]\\n    def norm(v):\\n        return math.sqrt(sum(x*x for x in v))\\n    def dot(a,b):\\n        return sum(x*y for x,y in zip(a,b))\\n    bl_norm = norm(blended)\\n    scores = []\\n    for vec in sv:\\n        vn = norm(vec)\\n        if bl_norm == 0 or vn == 0:\\n            scores.append(0.0)\\n        else:\\n            scores.append(dot(blended, vec) / (bl_norm * vn))\\n    return scores\"}\n\nBut need to escape backslashes: there is no backslash in the code except in the math.sqrt? Actually no backslash. Need to escape double quotes inside string: we have quotes around 'payload', etc. Since the JSON string is delimited by double quotes, we need to escape any double quote inside the string. In the Python code, we use single quotes for dict keys, so no double quotes inside. But we also have string literals like 'payload' – single quotes are fine in JSON string; they don't need escaping. Only double quotes, backslashes, and control characters. The code has no backslashes. So we can just put it as is, but we need to escape newlines as \\n and ensure no other issues.\n\nI'll replace each newline in the code with \\n. Also ensure there's no trailing newline at end of string? It's fine.\n\nLet me write the code as a single line with \\n. I'll copy the code and manually insert \\n.\n\nAlternatively, I can use a multiline string in JSON by including literal newlines, but JSON does not allow literal newlines in strings; they must be escaped as \\n. So I'll escape.\n\nI'll now write the final JSON.\n\nMake sure no extra spaces or formatting errors.\n\nI'll produce: {\"implementation\":\"import math\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    p = case['paylo